# rules: c-insecure-randomness
# languages: c
# fixture: c/CWE-330.c
# from: CWE-330 (Insufficiently Random Values), CWE-338 (Cryptographically Weak PRNG), CERT C MSC30-C, MSC32-C
#
# rand() and the drand48 family are linear congruential generators, and a
# linear congruential generator is a published recurrence: a handful of
# outputs recovers the state and the state gives every output after it. That
# is fine for a simulation and fatal for a token, a session id, a nonce or a
# temporary filename, and nothing in the call says which one this is - so the
# rule reports the generator and lets the reader say what it was for.
#
# The seed calls are here for a second reason. srand(time(NULL)) does not just
# use a weak generator, it starts it from a value the attacker also has, which
# makes the whole sequence reproducible rather than merely predictable. That
# is worth its own line even where the generator is only being used to pick a
# retry delay.
#
# RAND_pseudo_bytes is OpenSSL's own deprecated "may be predictable" entry
# point and sits with them. RAND_bytes, getrandom and arc4random are the ones
# that do not, so they are not reported.

["rand()", "rand_r($$$_)", "random()",
 "srand($$$_)", "srandom($$$_)",
 "drand48()", "erand48($$$_)", "lrand48()", "nrand48($$$_)",
 "mrand48()", "jrand48($$$_)", "srand48($$$_)", "seed48($$$_)",
 "lcong48($$$_)", "initstate($$$_)", "setstate($$$_)",
 "RAND_pseudo_bytes($$$_)"] as $weak
| scan_ast("*.[ch]"; $weak)
| finding("c-insecure-randomness";
    "this generator is a published recurrence, so its output is predictable "
    + "from its output; getrandom, arc4random_buf or RAND_bytes for anything "
    + "an attacker must not guess")
| report
